@page "/voice"

<CatHead Title="Voice">
    A spoken conversation with an agent. The app owns the microphone, the audio and the recogniser; the
    library paints what they report. <strong>One number connects them</strong>: the app writes
    <code>--level</code>, from 0 to 1, and the avatar, the <a href="/orb">orb</a>,
    <a href="/mochi">Mochi</a>, the bars and the mic button all move with it.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-voice-ai-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Writing the level</span>
        Read the loudness from an <code>AnalyserNode</code> on each animation frame, smooth it, and write it
        with <code>element.style.setProperty('--level', value)</code> on the session &mdash; once, on the
        element above everything that follows it. The examples here hold a fixed level so the page stays
        still. Under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> the bars and the button stand at the level
        instead of easing to it, and the orb holds a still frame.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Voice.Session" Title="A voice session" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.voice-session</code> centres the call: <code>.voice-stage</code> holds the one thing to look
        at &mdash; an <a href="/agent-avatar"><code>.agent-avatar--hero</code></a>, an
        <code>.orb--hero</code> or Mochi &mdash; then <code>.voice-who</code>, the
        <code>.voice-caption</code>, the bars and <code>.voice-controls</code>. The caption is
        <code>aria-live="polite"</code>, so what the agent says is also read out to a reader who cannot
        hear it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.voice-caption-interim</code> is the words the recogniser may still change: muted until they
        settle, so a correction does not look like the agent changing its mind.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Voice.Listening" Title="Listening" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        While the reader speaks, the stage shows the agent listening and the bars are
        <code>.voice-bars--mine</code>, in the brand colour, so it is clear whose voice is moving them.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Voice.MicButton" Title="The mic button" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.btn-mic</code> on any <code>.btn</code>, round. <code>data-state="listening"</code> fills it
        and rings it out with <code>--level</code>; <code>"muted"</code> paints it in danger. With no
        microphone, disable it and say why nearby. Change the <code>aria-label</code> with the state &mdash;
        it names what pressing does next.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Voice.Bars" Title="Live bars" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.voice-bars</code> is a live voice, as many <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> as fit; each bar has its
        own share of the level, so the row reads as a voice rather than a meter. It is decoration:
        <code>aria-hidden</code>. A recorded clip is <a href="/audio"><code>.audio-wave</code></a>, which
        draws a stored waveform rather than a live level.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Voice.Transcript" Title="The transcript" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        A <a href="/chat"><code>.chat--flat</code></a> beside the call, with the time into the call in
        <code>.chat-time</code>. The line still being recognised ends in
        <code>.voice-caption-interim</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>
